speechtext:
We discussed during this evening and a day or so ago the question oi the appointment of Mr. Chapais for the

control of the funds voted under the Agricultural Act of last year. As there might be some doubt in the minds of some hon. gentlemen as to the manner in which the federal money was administered by the Hon. Mr. Oaron, the Minister of Agriculture, I wish to put on 'Hansard' the following extract from the minutes of a meeting of the Council of Agriculture of the province of Quebec, held in the Legislative Building (room No. 54) in the city of Quebec at ten a.m. on Wednesday, the 19th day of March, 1913. The Council of Agriculture in Quebec, as in the other provinces, is a non-partisan body, and expresses the views of the most qualified amongst the farmers of the province. The extract is:
Moved by Mr. J. B. Carbonneau, seconded by Mr. A. J. Dawes and Hon. Mr. Garneau, and it is resolved:
Whereas this Council highly approves the manner in which the provincial Government has [DOT]employed the federal subsidy voted for the year 1912-13 in the interest of agriculture;
Whereas such employment has given full satisfaction to the farming class and has contributed to the improvement of agriculture and the development of several important agricultural industries;
Whereas the grants out of such subsidy for drainage, fruit-growing, dairying, poultry-raising, the cultivation of leguminous plants, stockbreeding, the preparation of smoked meats, the cultivation and curing of tobacco, the canning industry and the agricultural schools has had good results and will be productive of still better ones if such subsidies are continued for several years;
Whereas the mode of employment adopted this year under the agreement between the federal and provincial governments is more advantageous for the various needs of the province than if the subsidy were devoted to a single object determined by the federal Parliament;
This Council requests the federal Government to adopt legislation allowing the distribution of the $10,000,000 which it is proposed to give the provinces in the interest of agriculture to be so made as to continue to carry out the agricultural policy adopted at the last federal session.
The secretary of this Council is directed to send a copy of this resolution to Hon. Martin Burrell, Minister of Agriculture in the federal Government.
Oscar Lessard,
Secretary of the Council of Agriculture.
I put this on 'Hansard,' not in any controversial spirit, but just to remove any suspicion that might exist in the minds of some people as to the method in which the moneys given by the Dominion Government to the various provinces have been expended in the province of Quebec.
